---
/*
 * Two incompatible tracker transports behind one interface, with the announce
 * state machine above it unchanged. The interface line carries the accent because
 * it is the whole payoff: adding UDP meant one new class satisfying an existing
 * contract, with nothing upstream touched.
 *
 * Styling comes from the shared .diagram rules in src/styles/global.css.
 */
---

<figure class="diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="-8 -6 578 248"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="transports-title transports-desc"
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
  >
    <title id="transports-title">Two tracker transports behind one interface</title>
    <desc id="transports-desc">
      The announce state machine, with states stopped, connecting, waiting and error, sits above a
      single tracker interface. Two handlers implement that interface: an HTTP handler building a
      percent-escaped query and decoding a bencoded reply, and a UDP handler implementing BEP 15 with
      a hand-packed 98-byte payload. The state machine never learns which one it is talking to.
    </desc>

    <!-- The state machine, which is the thing that must not change. -->
    <g class="node">
      <rect x="130" y="16" width="300" height="52" rx="3" />
    </g>
    <g class="node-label">
      <text x="280" y="38" text-anchor="middle">announce state machine</text>
    </g>
    <g class="offset">
      <text x="280" y="56" text-anchor="middle">stopped · connecting · waiting · error</text>
    </g>

    <g class="collapse">
      <line x1="280" y1="68" x2="280" y2="86" />
      <polygon points="274,86 280,100 286,86" />
    </g>

    <!-- The single contract both transports satisfy. -->
    <g class="accent">
      <rect x="130" y="104" width="300" height="34" rx="3" />
      <text x="280" y="126" text-anchor="middle">one tracker interface</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Two implementations, neither known to the state machine. -->
    <g class="edge">
      <line x1="200" y1="138" x2="130" y2="166" />
      <line x1="360" y1="138" x2="430" y2="166" />
    </g>

    <g class="node">
      <rect x="0" y="170" width="252" height="52" rx="3" />
      <rect x="308" y="170" width="252" height="52" rx="3" />
    </g>
    <g class="node-label">
      <text x="126" y="192" text-anchor="middle">HTTP handler</text>
      <text x="434" y="192" text-anchor="middle">UDP handler, BEP 15</text>
    </g>
    <g class="offset">
      <text x="126" y="210" text-anchor="middle">percent-escaped, bencoded reply</text>
      <text x="434" y="210" text-anchor="middle">98 bytes at fixed offsets</text>
    </g>

    <g class="edge-label">
      <text x="0" y="12">never learns which transport it has</text>
    </g>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    Both transports satisfy one interface, so the announce state machine never learns which it is
    talking to. Adding the UDP transport meant writing a single class against an existing contract,
    with no change to the state machine at all.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
